(0) Many questions are the same across courses and across sections. CANVAS,=
 however, does not provide a method for making an announcement to all cours=
es and all sections.



(1) One is required to read the entire General Information Package to under=
stand how the course is structured and run. There are detailed sections on =
cutoffs for passing, failing, grading systems, studying, note-taking and so=
 on.



(2)  To put matters in perspective, please look at the Wikipedia articles o=
n every single topic. One will see that the notation used is very similar t=
o what I use in the notes. Please start with the Wikipedia Article on Sets.=
 Neither of the two recommended books is as strict in their use of language=
 or notation as required in this course. Nevertheless they are useful becau=
se they may be closer to what one is used to. As I have observed before, on=
e will gain perspective by reading them and one may get a general idea of w=
hat is going on. The Notes, however, define the standards of rigour to whic=
h one will be held. Therefore, one must read the Notes very carefully. One =
of the books uses the symbol for therefore below a conclusion bar which we =
shall never do. Points will be deducted if one does this.



(3) It is not necessary to solve every problem on an Exam to get a good gra=
de. It is, however, necessary to do every problem that one does do, complet=
ely correctly, and PROVE that one's solution is CORRECT, so that one may ge=
t full credit. Once one has finished this work for a particular problem, on=
e should copy it onto the fair copy immediately with every detail in place.=
 I have uploaded complete solutions to a and b problems from previous exams=
 to show how the work is to be presented. If one submits unchecked work, in=
complete work or unproved results, let one be warned that if the problem is=
 worth p points, then 2p points will be taken off. One needs to learn how t=
o solve problems in many different ways and one needs to be able to prove t=
hat one's solution is correct. One needs to submit only that which one can =
prove to be correct. Guesswork, gambles, and sloppiness will be severely pe=
nalized. This is the nature of mathematics and the sciences and this is exa=
ctly why I will never provide answers to homework problems which practice i=
s, to my mind, profoundly harmful.



(4) Problems 1a, 2a, 3a are the easy problems, Problems 1b, 2b, 3b are of i=
ntermediate difficulty, and Problems 1c, 2c, and 3c may be difficult. The G=
eneral Information Package discusses in detail what strategies to adopt to =
maximize the chances of getting a good grade. By Saturday of this week one =
must finish all the a's and at least two b's completely and have them copie=
d onto the fair copy.



(5) One should always consult the Notes and Solved Exams. IT IS CRUCIAL THA=
T ONE COMPUTE THE SETS DEFINED BY ONE'S J-NUMBERS CORRECTLY. IF THE SETS AR=
E INCORRECT, ONE WILL GET A ZERO.



(6) On Exam 1A,

Problem (1a) is a very simple problem similar to one that has been worked o=
ut. Please follow the procedure EXACTLY. STUDENTS OFTEN FORGET TO CIRCLE TH=
E CORRECT ITEMS IN ALL THE RELEVANT ROWS. PLEASE AVOID THIS ERROR.

One needs to check Y's and N's to see if the defining predicate is satisfie=
d. It is possible to have situations in which all the Y's are circled and n=
o N's and vice versa. One has to circle appropriate elemnts in one of the t=
op two rows, expect when the set defined in empty. The Solved Exam contains=
 such a problem. PLEASE MAKE SURE THAT YOUR SETS ARE CORRECTLY COMPUTED. If=
 one does not check, one is likely to lose points.

Problem (2a) is also very simple. It requires very careful reading.  Please=
 refer to the corresponding problem in the Solved Exam.

Problem (3a) is also a very simple problem. Please refer to the correspondi=
ng problem in the folder entitled Solved Exam.

Doing (1a) , (2a, (3a) completely correctly gives one 18 points on the exam=
 which standard, if consistently maintained throughout the course, is likel=
y to lead to a grade of C, provided one submits critiques and does enough p=
roblems from the Problem Set completely correctly.

Problem (1b) is similar to Problem (1a) . Please make sure that the answers=
 that are supposed to agree do agree and the check problems are done correc=
tly.  Otherwise no points.

Problem (2b) is on recursive specifications and requires some thought. It i=
s not difficult but it does test one's attention to detail. Please pay clos=
e attention. A mistake in any part propagates through the parts that follow=
.

Problem (3b) tests one's understanding of Sets and quantifiers.  DO NOT WRI=
TE NONSENSE DOWN. One should leave it blank unless one is certain that what=
 one is doing is completely correct.

If one does all the a's and b's completely correctly and submits all the cr=
itiques and an empty Problem Set, then one gets 36x6+ 30=3D246 which is cer=
tainly a B and only 9 points short of an A.



Problems (1c), (2c), and (3c)  test one's understanding of quantifiers and =
identities involving sets. If one succeeds in doing one of them correctly, =
yone will have done very well.

If one can do some of the c problems, well and good, if one cannot, one sho=
uld not despair.

