<td class=3D"maincell" style=3D"font-family:sans-serif; font-size:14px; ver=
tical-align:top; box-sizing:border-box; padding:20px;">
<p><span>(0) Many questions are the same across courses and across sections=
. CANVAS, however, does not provide a method for making an announcement to =
all courses and all sections.&nbsp;</span></p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><span>(1)&nbsp;<strong>One is required to read the entire General Inform=
ation Package to understand how the course is structured and run</strong>. =
There are detailed sections on cutoffs for passing, failing, grading system=
s, studying, note-taking and so on.&nbsp;</span></p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><span>(2) &nbsp;<strong>To put matters in perspective, please look at th=
e Wikipedia articles on every single topic. One will see that the notation =
used is very similar to what I use in the notes. Please start with the Wiki=
pedia Article on Sets.</strong></span><span>&nbsp;Neither
 of the two recommended books is as strict in their use of language or nota=
tion as required in this course. Nevertheless they are useful because they =
may be closer to what one is used to. As I have observed before, one will g=
ain perspective by reading them
 and one may get a general idea of what is going on. The Notes, however, de=
fine the standards of rigour to which one will be held. Therefore, one must=
 read the Notes very carefully. One of the books uses the symbol for theref=
ore below a conclusion bar which
 we shall never do. Points will be deducted if one does this.</span></p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><span>(3)&nbsp;<strong>It is not necessary to solve every problem on an =
Exam to get a good grade.&nbsp;It is, however, necessary to do every proble=
m that one&nbsp;<em>does</em>&nbsp;do, completely correctly, and PROVE that=
 one's solution is CORRECT, so that one may get full
 credit</strong>. Once one has finished this work for a particular problem,=
 one should copy it onto the fair copy immediately with every detail in pla=
ce.&nbsp;<strong>I have uploaded complete solutions to a and b problems fro=
m previous exams to show how the work
 is to be presented</strong>.</span><span>&nbsp;<strong>If one submits unch=
ecked work, incomplete work or unproved results, let one be warned that if =
the problem is worth p points, then 2p points will be taken off</strong>. O=
ne needs to learn how to solve problems
 in many different ways and one needs to be able to prove that one's soluti=
on is correct. One needs to submit only that which one can prove to be corr=
ect.&nbsp;<strong>Guesswork, gambles, and sloppiness will be severely penal=
ized</strong>. This is the nature of
 mathematics and the sciences and this is exactly why I will never provide&=
nbsp;</span><span>answers to homework problems which practice is, to my min=
d, profoundly harmful.&nbsp;</span></p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><span>(4) Problems 1a, 2a, 3a are the easy problems, Problems 1b, 2b, 3b=
 are of intermediate difficulty, and Problems 1c, 2c, and 3c may be difficu=
lt. The General Information Package discusses in detail what strategies to =
adopt to maximize the chances of
 getting a good grade.&nbsp;<strong>By Saturday of this week one must finis=
h all the a's and at least two b's completely and have them copied onto the=
 fair copy</strong>.&nbsp;</span></p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><span>(5) One should always consult the Notes and Solved Exams.&nbsp;<st=
rong>IT IS CRUCIAL THAT ONE COMPUTE THE SETS DEFINED BY ONE'S J-NUMBERS COR=
RECTLY. IF THE SETS ARE INCORRECT, ONE WILL GET A ZERO</strong>.</span></p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><span>(6)&nbsp;<strong>On Exam 1A</strong>,&nbsp;</span></p>
<p><span><strong>Problem (1a) is a very simple problem similar to one that =
has been worked out. Please follow the procedure EXACTLY.&nbsp;STUDENTS OFT=
EN FORGET TO CIRCLE THE CORRECT ITEMS IN ALL THE RELEVANT ROWS.&nbsp;PLEASE=
 AVOID THIS ERROR.</strong></span></p>
<p>One needs to check Y's and N's to see if the defining predicate is satis=
fied. It is possible to have situations in which all the Y's are circled an=
d no N's and vice versa. One has to circle appropriate elemnts in one of th=
e top two rows, expect when the
 set defined in empty. The Solved Exam contains such a problem.<span>&nbsp;=
</span><span><strong>PLEASE MAKE SURE THAT YOUR SETS ARE CORRECTLY COMPUTED=
</strong></span>.<span>&nbsp;</span><span><strong>If one does not check, on=
e is likely to lose points.</strong></span></p>
<p><span><strong>Problem (2a) is also very simple. It requires very careful=
 reading.&nbsp;</strong>&nbsp;Please refer to the corresponding problem in =
the Solved Exam.&nbsp;</span></p>
<p><span><strong>Problem (3a) is also a very simple problem.&nbsp;</strong>=
Please refer to the corresponding problem in the folder entitled Solved Exa=
m.&nbsp;</span></p>
<p><span><strong>Doing (1a) , (2a, (3a) completely correctly gives one 18 p=
oints on the exam which standard, if consistently maintained throughout the=
 course, is likely to lead to a grade of C, provided one submits critiques =
and does enough problems from the
 Problem Set completely correctly</strong>.</span></p>
<p><span><strong>Problem (1b) is similar to Problem (1a) .&nbsp;Please make=
 sure that the answers that are supposed to agree do agree and the check pr=
oblems are done correctly</strong>.&nbsp; Otherwise no points.</span></p>
<p><span><strong>Problem (2b) is on recursive specifica</strong></span><spa=
n><strong>tions and requires some thought.&nbsp;</strong>It is not difficul=
t but it does test one's attention to detail. Please pay close attention. A=
 mistake in any part propagates through
 the parts that follow.</span></p>
<p><span><strong>Problem (3b) tests one's understanding of Sets and quantif=
iers.&nbsp; DO NOT WRITE NONSENSE DOWN. One should leave it blank unless on=
e is certain that what one is doing is completely correct</strong>.</span><=
/p>
<p><span><strong>If one does all the a's and b's completely correctly and s=
ubmits all the critiques and an empty Problem Set, then one gets 36x6+ 30=
=3D246 which is certainly a B and only 9 points short of an A</strong>.</sp=
an></p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><span>Problems (1c), (2c), and (3c)&nbsp; test one's understanding of qu=
antifiers and identities involving sets. If one succeeds in doing one of th=
em correctly, yone will have done very well.<strong></strong></span></p>
<p><span><strong>If one can do some of the c problems, well and good, if on=
e cannot, one should not despair</strong>.</span></p>
